      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg/day, which is double the maximum labeled daily dose of oral ibuprofen for adults (3,200 mg/day under supervision, and 1,200 mg/day for OTC use). A dose that high raises the risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nHere are some common regimens, in case the intended dose was different:\n- **400 mg PO every 6 hours** (1,600 mg/day) is typical for mild to moderate pain or fever.\n- **600 mg PO every 6 hours** (2,400 mg/day) or **800 mg PO three times daily** (2,400 mg/day) are typical for inflammatory conditions or moderate pain.\n- **800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day) is the maximum dose. It's used for conditions like arthritis, and only when a prescriber has decided it's warranted.\n\n1600 mg may have been meant as the total daily dose, or the order may have been mistyped.\n\nOrders also normally come from the treating prescriber. If the prescriber confirms the intended dose, I can enter it right away. If you want to go ahead with one of the regimens above, tell me which one and confirm that the treating clinician has approved it."
      }
